    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462271</IRI>
        <Literal>GitHub, Inc., is an Internet hosting service for software development and version control using Git. It provides the distributed version control of Git plus access control, bug tracking, software feature requests, task management, continuous integration, and wikis for every Project collabration.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622710</IRI>
        <Literal>CASTEP psEuropedopotential strings and data for the elements</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622711</IRI>
        <Literal>PubChem is the world&apos;s largest collection of freely accessible chemical information. Search chemicals by name, molecular formula, structure, and other identifiers. Find chemical and physical properties, biological activities, safety and toxicity information, patents, literature citations and more.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622712</IRI>
        <Literal>Bilbao Crystallographic Server is an open access website offering online crystallographic Database Software and programs aimed at analyzing, calculating and visualizing problems of structural and mathematical crystallography, solid state physics and structural chemistry.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622713</IRI>
        <Literal>Open-access collection of crystal structures of organic, inorganic, metal-organic compounds and minerals, excluding biopolymers.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622714</IRI>
        <Literal>By computing properties of all known materials, the Materials Project collabration aims to remove guesswork from materials design in a variety of applications. Experimental research can be targeted to the most promising compounds from computational data sets. Researchers will be able to data-mine scientific trends in materials properties. By providing materials researchers with the information they need to design better, the Materials Project collabration aims to accelerate innovation in materials research.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622715</IRI>
        <Literal>Materials Cloud is built to enable the seamless sharing and dissemination of resources in computational materials science, offering educational, research, and archiving tools; simulation software and services; and curated and raw data. These underpin published results and empower data-based discovery, compliant with data management plans and the FAIR principles.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622716</IRI>
        <Literal>The Novel Materials Discovery (NOMAD) Laboratory maintains the largest worldwide repository for input and output files of all important computational materials science computer programs.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622717</IRI>
        <Literal>Ontology repository for materials science.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622718</IRI>
        <Literal>This website provides an interactive interface for the assessment of interatomic potentials presented in OpenKIM, NIST IPR and elsewhere with respect to DFT reference data. The calculations were carried out using the pyiron workflow management tool.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622719</IRI>
        <Literal>Automatic FLOW (AFLOW) Database Software for computational materials science. Globally available Database Software of 3,528,653 material compounds with over 733,959,824 calculated properties, and growing.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462272</IRI>
        <Literal>Zenodo is a general-purpose open repository developed under the Europeropean OpenAIRE program and operated by CERN. It allows researchers to deposit research papers, data sets, research software, reports, and any other research related digital artefacts.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622720</IRI>
        <Literal>JARVIS (Joint Automated Repository for Various Integrated Simulations) is a repository designed to automate materials discovery and optimization using classical force-field, density functional theory, machine learning calculations and experiments.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622721</IRI>
        <Literal>Online materials Database Software (known as PAULING FILE Project collabration) with nearly 2 million entries: physical properties, crystal structures, phase diagrams, available via API, ready for modern data-intensive applications. The source of these entries are about 300,000 peer-reviewed Publications in materials science, processed during the last 16 years by an international team of PhD editors. The results are presented online with a quick search interface. The basic access is provided for free.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622722</IRI>
        <Literal>The Open Materials Database Software is a Publicly available, searchable Database Software for information on the properties of materials created by people who work in computational materials design research.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622723</IRI>
        <Literal>The OQMD is a Database Software of DFT calculated thermodynamic and structural properties of 1,022,603 materials, created in Chris Wolverton&apos;s group at Northwestern University.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622724</IRI>
        <Literal>Citrination is the premier open Database Software and analytics platform for the world&apos;s material and chemical information. Developed by Citrine Informatics.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622725</IRI>
        <Literal>MatDB is a Database Software application for experimentally measured engineering materials data. It supports open, registered, and restricted access. It presently hosts more than 20.000 unique data sets coming mainly from Europeropean and Member State research programmes. It supports web interfaces for entering, browsing, and retrieving data. MatDB is also enabled for innovative services, including data citation and interoperability standards. The data citation service relies on DataCite DOIs.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622726</IRI>
        <Literal>Over 1000 detailed, fully referenced and verified datasets for steels, aluminium and titanium alloys, cast irons/steels, weld metals. Materials can be searched according to a number of different criteria. Initial search results are presented in the form of a table from which they can be selected for presentation in form of detailed report or for comparison overview (up to 5 materials). In addition to material information and values of properties/parameters, images of microstructure, specimens and those of stress-strain, stress- and strain-life curves (if available) can be reviewed as well.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622727</IRI>
        <Literal>MDR is a data repository to collect and store papers, presentation materials, and related materials data to accumulate and release them in a form suitable for the promotion of materials research and materials informatics. Users can search the documents and the data from information (metadata) such as sample, instrument, method, and from the full text of the deposited data, to browse and download them freely.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622728</IRI>
        <Literal>The NIMS Materials Database Software (MatNavi) aims to contribute to the development of new materials and the selection of materials. MatNavi includes Polymer DB (chemical structures, polymerization, processing, physical properties, NMR spectra, etc.), Inorganic MaterialDB (crystal structures, phase diagrams, physical properties, etc.), Metallic Material DB (density, elastic constants, creep characteristics, etc.) and Computational Electronic Structure DB (band structures obtained by first-principles calculations, etc). It also offers applications such as the Composite Design &amp; Property Prediction System. If you register, you can search and browse various Database Softwares for free.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622729</IRI>
        <Literal>Polymer Database Software &quot;PoLyInfo&quot; systematically provides various data required for polymeric material design. The main data source is academic literature on polymers. Information on polymers including properties, chemical structures, IUPAC names, processing methods of measured samples, measurement conditions, used monomers and polymerization methods are stored in a object Database Software. About 100 types of properties including thermal, electrical and mechanical properties are covered. Homopolymers, copolymers, furthermore polymer blends, composites and compounds that consist of homopolymers and copolymers are open to the Public.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462273</IRI>
        <Literal>Dryad is an international open-access repository of research data, especially data underlying scientific and medical Publications. Dryad is a curated general-purpose repository that makes data discoverable, freely rEuropesable, and citable.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622730</IRI>
        <Literal>The Inorganic Material Database Software aims to cover all basic crystal structure, x-ray diffraction, property and phase diagram data of inorganic and metallic materials from main literature sources.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622731</IRI>
        <Literal>CompES-X is a Database Software of electronic structures predicted by the first-principles calculations for mono-element and multi-element crystalline inorganic compounds that are based on the experimental data of crystal structures (CIF file) of a broad range of materials collected in Inorganic Materials Database Software (AtomWork). The calculations are carried out mainly by using the first-principles plane wave psEuropedopotential method as implemented in VASP code. The entry of calculated electronic structure data is in one-to-one association with that of experimental data of crystal structures of inorganic compounds contained in AtomWork.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622732</IRI>
        <Literal>The Diffusion Database Software aims to cover all the basic diffusion data of metallic and inorganic materials mainly from the literature sources. The Database Software consists of &quot;diffusion data&quot; and &quot;reference data&quot;. Currently the system primarily contains information of pure metals, alloys, semiconductors, ceramics and intermetallics.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622733</IRI>
        <Literal>This Database Software contains data on density, surface tension and viscosity coefficient of metals, alloys and ceramics obtained by floating experiments using an electrostatic levitation furnace.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622734</IRI>
        <Literal>The Metallic Materials Database Software (Kinzoku) includes the density, elastic modulus, poisson ratio, hardness, toughness, tensile, creep, creep rupture properties, and various fatigue properties of structural metallic materials. It can be searched by JIS standard number, ASTM standard number, material name, material form, etc.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622735</IRI>
        <Literal>The welding Database Software system to predict hardness and microstructural constituents in the heat affected zone and to determine optimum welding conditions has been developed by using an estimated thermal cycle and the Database Software of CCT diagram for welding.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622736</IRI>
        <Literal>The comprehensive repository of validated and curated small-molecule organic and metal-organic crystal structures.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622737</IRI>
        <Literal>ICSD is an information service in crystallography and comprises the worldâs largest Database Software for fully identified inorganic crystal structures.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622738</IRI>
        <Literal>Each CMR Project collabration consists of an ASE-Database Software and a Project collabration page describing the data and showing examples of how to work with the data using Python and ASE.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622739</IRI>
        <Literal>The electronic structure Project collabration (ESP) is a collection of some 60,000 electronic structure calculations. A full-potential linear muffin-tin orbital (FP-LMTO) implementation of density functional theory (DFT) within the local density approximation (LDA) was used to solve the electronic structures.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462274</IRI>
        <Literal>OSF is a free, open platform to support research data and enable collaboration.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622740</IRI>
        <Literal>The HybriDÂ³ materials Database Software provides a comprehensive collection of experimental and computational materials data for crystalline organic-inorganic compounds, predominantly based on the perovskite paradigm.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622741</IRI>
        <Literal>A platform to store structure and property data created by atomistic simulations, and toold to design materials by learning from the data</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622742</IRI>
        <Literal>An informatics platform for polymer property prediction and design using machine learning</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622743</IRI>
        <Literal>A simple way to publish, discover, and access materials datasets</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622744</IRI>
        <Literal>The Material Properties Open Database Software (MPOD) is a web-based, open access repository of quantitative information about the physical properties of crystalline materials.  MPOD is oriented to design engineers, scientists, science teachers and students. Properties are generally treated as tensor magnitudes. In MPOD the compact matrix notation is applied. To bring an intuitive view of tensor properties, so-called longitudinal properties surfaces are displayed.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622745</IRI>
        <Literal>nanoHUB.org is the premier place for computational nanotechnology research, education, and collaboration. Our site hosts a rapidly growing collection of Simulation Programs for nanoscale phenomena that run in the cloud and are accessible through a web browser.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622746</IRI>
        <Literal>NRELMatDB is a computational materials Database Software with the specific focus on materials for renewable energy applications including, but not limited to, photovoltaic materials, materials for photo-electrochemical water splitting, thermoelectrics, etc. The main goal of NRELMatDB is to enable and facilitate the access and exchange of computational data between different research groups following the guidelines outlined in the Presidential Materials Genome Initiative (http://www.mgi.gov).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622747</IRI>
        <Literal>The organic materials Database Software is an open access electronic structure Database Software for 3-dimensional organic crystals, developed and hosted at the Nordic Institute for Theoretical Physics â Nordita.  It provides tools for search queries based on data-mining and machine learning techniques. The universal features provided on our web interface facilitate the design of novel functional organic materials with a wide-range of applications.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622748</IRI>
        <Literal>Itâs getting to be a mainstream idea collecting a big set of ab-initio calculations and analyzing them by informatics approach. For scientific purpose, we plan to do it for phonon properties. As an attempt, we have run a set of ab-initio phonon calculations and left the raw data on this static web site. The aim of this web site is just leaving the data to distribute.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622749</IRI>
        <Literal>Open-access collection of Raman spectra used for the SOLSA H2020 Project collabration.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462275</IRI>
        <Literal>figshare is a repository where users can make all of their research outputs available in a citable, shareable and discoverable manner</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622750</IRI>
        <Literal>Using a recently developed formalism called topological quantum chemistry, we perform a high-throughput search of âhigh-qualityâ materials (for which the atomic positions and structure have been measured very accurately) in the Inorganic Crystal Structure Database Software in order to identify new topological phases. We develop codes to compute all characters of all symmetries of 26,938 stoichiometric materials, and find 3,307 topological insulators, 4,078 topological semimetals and no fragile phases. For these 7,385 materials we provide the electronic band structure, including some electronic properties (bandgap and number of electrons), symmetry indicators, and other topological information. Our results show that more than 27 per cent of all materials in nature are topological. We provide an open-source code that checks the topology of any material and allows other researchers to reproduce our results.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622751</IRI>
        <Literal>Welcome to Carolina Materials Database Software created in Jianjun Hu&apos;s and Ming Hu&apos;s groups at University of South Carolina. A freely, Globally accessible Database Software of 38,716 inorganic material compounds with over 80,000 calculated properties, and growing.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622752</IRI>
        <Literal>CascadesDB is a Database Software of molecular dynamics simulations of collision cascades, developed by the International Atomic Energy Agency. The Project collabration was initiated by the 5th meeting of the IAEA&apos;s Code Centres Network in November 2017 to enhance the usage and long-term curation of collisional cascade simulations for the analysis and prediction of radiation damage in materials for fusion and fission applications.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622753</IRI>
        <Literal>The Surface Reactions Database Software contains thousands of reaction energies and barriers from density functional theory (DFT) calculations on surface systems.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622754</IRI>
        <Literal>IRIC Database Software (Information Resources on Inorganic Chemistry) contains brief information on information systems on inorganic chemistry and material science. All data are taken from Publications.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622755</IRI>
        <Literal>Information system to facilitate research of domain specific repositories and Database Softwares, as well as datasets published individually by research groups</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622756</IRI>
        <Literal>A site for Materials Scientists to collaborate, store and publish research. The Materials Commons is supported by the U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Basic Energy Sciences, Division of Materials Sciences and Engineering.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622757</IRI>
        <Literal>ThermoML is an XML-based IUPAC Standard for Storage Service and exchange of thermophysical and thermochemical property data.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622758</IRI>
        <Literal>This site hosts the GBRV psEuropedopotential library, a highly accurate and computationally inexpensive open-source psEuropedopotential library which has been designed and optimized for use in high-throughput DFT calculations and released under the gnu Public license.  We provide potential files for direct use with the Quantum Espresso, Abinit, and JDFTx plane-wave psEuropedopotential codes, as well as input files for the Vanderbilt Ultrasoft psEuropedopotential generator.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/174532124622759</IRI>
        <Literal>The National Center for Biomedical Ontology was founded as one of the National Centers for Biomedical Computing, supported by the NHGRI, the NHLBI, and the NIH Common Fund under grant U54-HG004028.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462276</IRI>
        <Literal>This repository provides a source for interatomic potentials (force fields), related files, and evaluation tools to help researchers obtain interatomic models and judge their quality and applicability.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462277</IRI>
        <Literal>OpenKIM is a curated repository of interatomic potentials and analytics for making classical molecular simulations of materials reliable, reproducible, and accessible. Content on OpenKIM is open source and freely available.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462278</IRI>
        <Literal>Atomistica is a library of interatomic potentials. It is designed to be plugged into different simulation environments.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453212462279</IRI>
        <Literal>This is the prototype interface for the CCP-NC Database Software of calculated NMR data.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453216178851</IRI>
        <Literal>EDN</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>https://nfdi.fiz-karlsruhe.de/matwerk/msekg/17453216178852</IRI>
        <Literal>smiles</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
